# A leakage-aware knowledge graph and benchmark for herb–drug interaction prediction in Ayurveda: what works, what does not, and a composition case study

> **DRAFT for internal and mentor review. Not submitted.** Every number below is generated from the project's result files by `python -m ayurveda_kg.paper`; do not edit numbers by hand. Items marked **[VERIFY]** are references or statements that must be checked against the primary source before submission. The gold set (12 pairs) is pending review by a pharmacist or pharmacology expert.

## Abstract

Many people in India take Ayurvedic herbal preparations together with prescription drugs, yet no accessible tool checks the combination the way drug–drug interaction checkers do. We build an open, source-attributed knowledge graph linking {{kg_herbs}} commonly co-used herbs, {{kg_compounds|int}} of their compounds, {{kg_targets|int}} protein-target genes and {{kg_drugs}} commonly co-prescribed drugs, and use it to study herb–drug interaction (HDI) prediction. Because no large open HDI benchmark exists, we derive mechanistic *silver* labels at the compound–drug level ({{lab_pos|int}} positive of {{lab_pairs|int}} pairs: a compound predicted to inhibit a cytochrome P450 enzyme of which the drug is a substrate) and evaluate against a hand-verified literature *gold* set of {{gold_n}} pairs. Our central methodological finding is that the label-defining edges must be removed from the training graph: a Random Forest given the unmasked enzyme features scores up to {{p4_leak_cold_herb|f3}} AUROC higher than the same model on the masked graph, an inflation that cold splits alone do not prevent. On leak-free cold-compound, cold-herb and cold-drug splits, a Random Forest on compound chemistry and non-enzyme targets reaches AUROC {{p4_cold_compound_rf_auroc|f3}}, {{p4_cold_herb_rf_auroc|f3}} and {{p4_cold_drug_rf_auroc|f3}}; a heterogeneous graph neural network does not beat it, and it differs from the identical network without message passing by only {{p4_gnn_minus_mlp_cold_compound|f3}} AUROC on cold compounds (GNN minus MLP), so graph structure adds little beyond node features in this setting. A pooled cold-drug AUROC of {{p4_cold_drug_rf_auroc|f3}} is misleading: ranking unseen drugs for a given compound reaches only {{p4_cold_drug_rf_within_compound|f3}} (random forest) and {{p4_cold_drug_gnn_within_compound|f3}} (GNN; 0.5 is chance). The silver labels inherit known false alarms, which a literature gold set exposes. Finally, a linear-programming case study re-weights {{p5_forms_opt}} real formulations to lower predicted risk, with a modest median reduction of {{p5_median_red|pct1}}. We release code and identifiers (not IMPPAT-derived tables, for licence reasons).

## 1. Introduction

Co-use of Ayurvedic and conventional treatment is common. A 2025 analysis of the Longitudinal Ageing Study in India reported that 6.68% of adults aged 45 and over used AYUSH therapies alongside conventional care in the past year, and 7.78% of people treated for diabetes did so **[VERIFY: figures taken from the project brief; confirm against the LASI paper]**. Herbs contain pharmacologically active compounds that can inhibit or induce drug-metabolising enzymes or transporters and so change a drug's exposure, but a prescriber rarely sees the herbal product and an Ayurvedic practitioner rarely sees the full prescription list.

We ask three questions. (i) Can a source-attributed graph built only from open data support HDI prediction for a fixed, clinically motivated scope? (ii) How should labels be constructed and evaluated so that a model is not simply rediscovering the labelling rule? (iii) Does graph structure help beyond compound chemistry and target annotations? Our contributions are: a scoped open knowledge graph; a label and evaluation design with explicit leakage control and a quantified inflation; a benchmark with honest negative results (no graph benefit, weak drug-side generalisation); a small literature gold set that exposes the labels' false alarms; and a composition case study on real formulations.

## 2. Related work

Graph models have been applied to herb–target and herb–herb or TCM drug–drug prediction, for example HTINet2 (knowledge-graph embedding with a residual-like graph neural network; Brief. Bioinform. 2024;25(5):bbae414), MAMGN-HTI (metapaths with attention for herb–target interaction; PMC12561231) and a dual graph attention network for TCM drug–drug interaction (Sci. Rep. 2025, s41598-025-00725-9). A decision-support system for herb–drug interactions has also been described (arXiv:2306.15365). We are not aware of an equivalent open resource for Ayurveda built on IMPPAT 2.0 (ACS Omega 2023, doi:10.1021/acsomega.3c00156) **[VERIFY: confirm this gap with a systematic search before submission]**. Our data sources are IMPPAT 2.0, ChEMBL, DGIdb 5.0 (Nucleic Acids Res. 2024;52:D1227), DDInter 2.0 (PMC11701621) and the CYP substrate labels of Carbon-Mangels and Hutter as distributed by the Therapeutics Data Commons (arXiv:2102.09548) **[VERIFY: full author lists, years and the ChEMBL and SwissADME citations]**.

## 3. Data and knowledge graph

**Scope (fixed in advance, not expanded).** {{kg_herbs}} herbs commonly co-used in India or with documented interaction concern, and {{kg_drugs}} drugs across the classes people on AYUSH therapies commonly take (anticoagulants and antiplatelets, antidiabetics, antihypertensives and diuretics, statins, thyroid, immunosuppressants, anticonvulsants and others).

**Graph.** Node types are herb, compound, target gene and drug. The graph has {{kg_edges_contains|int}} herb–compound edges, {{kg_edges_modulates|int}} compound–target edges, {{kg_edges_predicted_cyp_inhibitor|int}} predicted CYP-inhibition edges, {{kg_edges_targets|int}} drug–target edges, {{kg_edges_substrate_of}} drug–enzyme substrate and {{kg_edges_non_substrate_of}} verified non-substrate edges, and {{kg_edges_ddi}} drug–drug interactions. Every edge keeps its source. Many compound–target and CYP-inhibition edges are in-silico predictions (SwissADME as presented by IMPPAT), which the model and the analysis can distinguish from experimental evidence. Compounds are keyed by InChIKey, herbs resolved through aliases (for example IMPPAT files *Tinospora cordifolia* under *Tinospora sinensis*), and drugs to their ChEMBL parent molecule with an alias table that refuses ambiguous aliases. A validator checks duplicates, dangling edges and type mismatches, and real-data plausibility tests check known pharmacology (for example that black pepper contains piperine and that licorice has the most predicted CYP3A4 inhibitors).

## 4. Labels and leakage control

**Silver labels (compound–drug).** For a compound with SwissADME predictions and a drug, the label is positive if the compound is predicted to inhibit a CYP enzyme (among CYP1A2, 2C9, 2C19, 2D6, 3A4) of which the drug is a known substrate; negative if, for every enzyme the compound is predicted to inhibit, the drug is a verified non-substrate (or the compound inhibits none); and left **unlabelled** when the drug's substrate status is unknown. This yields {{lab_pos|int}} positive, {{lab_neg|int}} negative and {{lab_unl|int}} unlabelled pairs ({{lab_pos_compounds|int}} compounds with at least one positive).

**The circularity problem.** The label is a deterministic function of graph edges (predicted inhibition and substrate status). A model that sees those edges can recover the rule. We therefore (a) remove all label-source edges and any edge into the five enzymes from the training graph, with a test showing the labels cannot be re-derived from what remains, and (b) use cold splits in which test compounds, herbs or drugs never appear in training; each fold's training graph excludes the held-out nodes entirely. Compounds shared between a held-out herb and training herbs are dropped, not guessed.

**Gold set.** {{gold_n}} herb–drug pairs from published human studies and reviews, each with citation, mechanism type and evidence level, read from the primary PubMed record (or full-text passage) rather than from memory; {{gold_pk_pos}} are pharmacokinetic positives and {{gold_neg}} are trial negatives. It is used only for evaluation, supports a qualitative case study rather than statistical claims, and is pending expert review.

## 5. Models and evaluation

We compare a per-drug prior; matrix factorisation (reported only on the inflated random-pair reference, because it cannot represent unseen compounds); a Random Forest on a Morgan fingerprint of the compound, its non-enzyme target profile, a predicted P-glycoprotein flag and drug features (non-enzyme targets, class, drug–drug interaction profile); a heterogeneous GraphSAGE link predictor with inductive node features; the same network with no message passing (an MLP ablation); and the Random Forest given the unmasked enzyme features (a leakage ablation). We report AUROC and AUPRC over 5 folds (GNN and MLP over 3 seeds), plus within-drug AUROC (ranking compounds for one drug: compound-side skill) and within-compound AUROC (ranking drugs for one compound: drug-side skill), because pooled AUROC can be driven by either.

## 6. Results

**Leakage inflates scores.** The unmasked Random Forest exceeds the masked one by {{p4_leak_cold_compound|f3}} (cold-compound), {{p4_leak_cold_herb|f3}} (cold-herb) and {{p4_leak_cold_drug|f3}} (cold-drug) AUROC.

**Compound-side skill is real, with a caveat.** The Random Forest reaches AUROC {{p4_cold_compound_rf_auroc|f3}} ± {{p4_cold_compound_rf_auroc_std|f3}} on cold compounds and {{p4_cold_herb_rf_auroc|f3}} ± {{p4_cold_herb_rf_auroc_std|f3}} on cold herbs (fold range {{p4_cold_herb_rf_auroc_min|f3}} to {{p4_cold_herb_rf_auroc_max|f3}}), against {{p4_cold_compound_prior_auroc|f3}} and {{p4_cold_herb_prior_auroc|f3}} for the prior. Because the silver labels derive from structure-based predictions, this is best read as learning structure-to-predicted-activity rather than new biology.

**Graph structure adds little.** The GNN scores {{p4_cold_compound_gnn_auroc|f3}} and {{p4_cold_herb_gnn_auroc|f3}} on cold compounds and herbs; the no-message-passing MLP scores {{p4_cold_compound_mlp_auroc|f3}} and {{p4_cold_herb_mlp_auroc|f3}}. The Random Forest is ahead of the GNN on every cold split, and five training configurations of the GNN, tried on two folds, gave validation AUROCs within about 0.005 of one another, so this does not look like an under-training artefact (a diagnostic, not a full hyperparameter search).

**Drug-side generalisation is weak.** Pooled cold-drug AUROC is {{p4_cold_drug_rf_auroc|f3}} for the Random Forest, but ranking unseen drugs for a given compound gives {{p4_cold_drug_rf_within_compound|f3}} (Random Forest) and {{p4_cold_drug_gnn_within_compound|f3}} (GNN), against 0.5 for chance; the pooled number is almost entirely compound-side skill (within-drug AUROC {{p4_cold_drug_rf_within_drug|f3}}).

**False alarms against the gold set.** The silver labels, and the models trained on them, score ginger with warfarin among the highest herb–drug pairs although a human crossover trial found no effect on warfarin pharmacokinetics or pharmacodynamics; they correctly miss the pharmacodynamic licorice–diuretic interactions, which a CYP-based rule cannot represent. Predicted CYP inhibition by compounds is a mechanistic hypothesis, not a clinical interaction.

## 7. Composition case study

Using the cached out-of-fold risk scores, a linear program re-weights the scoped herbs of {{p5_forms_opt}} real IMPPAT formulations (of {{p5_forms|int}} parsed, {{p5_forms_scoped}} containing at least one scoped herb) for a patient's drug, keeping each herb's share within a factor of its baseline and each therapeutic use above a coverage floor. IMPPAT lists ingredients but no proportions, so the baseline is an assumed equal split. Over {{p5_scenarios|int}} scenarios all solved, coverage never fell below the floor ({{p5_min_coverage|f2}}), and the median relative risk reduction was {{p5_median_red|pct1}} (90th percentile {{p5_p90_red|pct1}}); looser constraints raise the median to {{p5_sweep_max_red|pct1}}, and random baselines give {{p5_random_median|pct1}} against {{p5_equal_median|pct1}} for equal parts. {{p5_transfer_improve_frac|pct}} of suggestions also improved under an independent risk estimate (Spearman {{p5_spearman|f2}}). A direction check against the published studies shows the optimiser lowers the share of the herbs with published pharmacokinetic interactions in {{p5_dir_pos_mean|pct}} of scenarios on average (per pair {{p5_dir_pos_min|pct}} to {{p5_dir_pos_max|pct}}), but it also lowers herbs whose trials found no interaction in {{p5_dir_neg_mean|pct}} of scenarios. The output is a hypothesis, not a dosing recommendation.

## 8. Limitations

- Labels are mechanistic hypotheses derived partly from in-silico predictions; they have demonstrated false alarms and the independent experimental label source (ChEMBL CYP bioactivity) is future work.
- Only {{kg_herbs}} herbs, {{kg_drugs}} drugs and five CYP enzymes are covered; no UGT or transporter label logic; pharmacodynamic interactions are out of reach for a CYP-based rule.
- A large share of compounds have no target annotation; the graph is sparse on the compound side.
- The gold set has {{gold_n}} pairs, only {{gold_neg}} of them negatives, and is pending expert review.
- IMPPAT is licensed CC BY-NC-ND; we release code and identifiers only and will request permission from the database authors before releasing any derived tables.
- The composition baseline is an assumption (equal parts) and compounds are weighted equally within a herb because abundance data are unavailable.

## 9. Conclusion

An open, scoped Ayurvedic knowledge graph supports compound-side prediction of mechanistic herb–drug interaction candidates, but careful label construction matters more than model class: removing label-source edges changes reported AUROC by up to {{p4_leak_cold_herb|f3}}, graph message passing adds little over node features, and generalisation to unseen drugs is weak. We view the benchmark, the leakage protocol and the gold-set comparison as the main contributions, and the label source and the gold set as the main things to strengthen next.

